          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Be the first to 'Go Out' by melding all cards in your hand and have the lowest total score after 9 hands.",
          gameplay: '1. **Contracts**: Each hand has a specific contract to fulfill before melding.\n2. **The Call**: Any player can call for a discard out of turn. If accepted, they must take the discard PLUS a penalty card from the stock. (Limit: 3 calls per hand).\n3. **Melds**: Threes (sets) and Fours (runs). Jokers are wild.\n4. **Pointing**: Jokers (25), Black Aces (15), K-Q-J-10 (10), 2-9 (Face), Red Aces (1). Lowest total wins.',
          keyRules: [
            'Hand 1: 3 Threes (Sets)',
            'Hand 2: 2 Threes, 1 Four (Run)',
            'Hand 3: 2 Fours, 1 Three',
            'Hand 4: 3 Fours',
            'Hand 5: 4 Threes',
            'Hand 6: 3 Threes, 1 Four',
            'Hand 7: 2 Threes, 2 Fours',
            'Hand 8: 1 Three, 3 Fours',
            'Hand 9: 4 Fours',
            'The Call: Take discard out of turn with penalty card (max 3 calls)',
            'Jokers are wild but cannot be consecutive in a run',
            'Must have at least two natural cards in a set',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Don't 'Call' cards in the early rounds unless it completes your Eye/Set. The penalty cards accumulate fast and can leave you with too many points if you don't go out first.",
          intermediate: "Watch the 'Aces'. Black Aces (15) are dangerous to hold, but Red Aces (1) are excellent for lowering your point total if you are caught with cards at the end.",
          advanced: "The 'Bending' Rule: If you go 'Down and Out' (meld your entire hand in one turn), your opponents score double points for their remaining cards. Try to time your melds to achieve this.",
          tips: [
            'Count the Jokers! If 3 are on the table, the 4th is a high-value target to call.',
            'In the 16-card final round, hoarding low-value cards (2, 3, 4) is better than chasing high-value sets.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
